---
description: Write Git commit messages as operations on the codebase; preserve intent for review, bisect, and revert
alwaysApply: true
---

# Commit messages are debugging infrastructure

A commit is an operation that can be applied, reverted, cherry-picked, and bisected. History is for review, debug, and revert — not a diary of the author's day.

The subject completes "If applied, this commit will …". Use the imperative mood: "Add X", not "Added X" or "I added X". Name the meaningful change. Do not prefix with `fix:` / `chore:` — a category is not intent.

The body explains why when the diff does not. Do not narrate the patch. Vague subjects usually mean the commit mixed unrelated work: split until one purpose is easy to name. Do not record workflow ("WIP", "fix lint", "address comments").

```text
# ❌ Past tense / diary / workflow
Added retry for Alpaca submit
WIP
fix lint
Address PR feedback

# ❌ Repeats the diff
Change reconnect timeout from 5000 to 10000

# ✅ Imperative: what applying the commit does
Retry Alpaca submit after transient reject
Reject flip when buying power is insufficient

# ✅ Body when why is not in the diff
Increase reconnect timeout for slow quote streams

Mobile quote streams stalled before the previous window,
so the client looked connected while every request failed.
```
